WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.639 --> 00:00:03.830 align:start position:0%
 
a clash at sea  causing waves of international concern a  russian military ship rams into a  ukrainian tugboat off the coast of  crimea  the vessel was later fired at and  captured by the russians along with two  other ships  and dozens of ukrainian seamen several  of whom were injured  the united nations security council held  an emergency meeting  where western countries accused russia  of stoking an already deadly war with  ukraine  what we witnessed this weekend is yet  another reckless  russian escalation it is an arrogant act  that the international community must  condemn and will never  accept in response russia's  representative said the ukrainian ship  had violated  its territorial waters moscow says the  incident was a provocation by ukrainian  president petro poroshenko  to boost his support in upcoming  elections  in kiev poroshenko has declared a state  of martial law for 30 days  but insisted it would not interfere with  the looming presidential vote  in brussels nato secretary general  warned russia that its actions would  have consequences  there is no justification for the use of  military force  against ukrainian ships and naval  personnel  so we call on russia to release  immediately  the ukrainian sailors and ships it  ceased  yesterday and we call for  calm and restraint  sea traffic is again flowing through the  disputed waters  after russia briefly blocked the crucial  passage  but moscow says it will continue to hold  the ukrainian ships and sailors  while it conducts its own investigation  this outrageous violation of sovereign  ukrainian territory  is part of a pattern of russian behavior  that includes the purported annexation  of crimea  and abuses against countless ukrainians  in crimea  as well as stoking conflict that has  taken the lives  of more than 10 000 people in eastern  ukraine  further escalation of the situation by  the russian federation should not be  ruled out  according to available intelligence data  there is a clear threat for invasion  and seizing of marijuana and bergensk  further aggressive acts and provocations  by the fsb  border guard service and russian navy  remains a realistic possibility  what kiev has been doing in coordination  with the united states and others  to promote a conflict line against  russia is fraught with very dangerous  consequences  we are going to curb any infringement of  russian sovereignty and territorial  integrity  including the sea of azov and that area  we do hope that is clear  well for more on this story we're joined  now by our dw correspondent nick  connolly in kiev  and emily sherwin in moscow good morning  to you both  nick ukraine has imposed martial law  what kinds of power  does this give ukrainian authorities and  how will that help them solve this  crisis  good morning terry well this is  unprecedented in all the years of  conflict we've seen here in ukraine  after the annexation by russia of crimea  and the conflict in the east this has  never happened before this is  a first um basically this gives the  government the option to restrict civil  liberties anything from freedom of  speech  to freedom of assembly giving the police  more powers and also potentially  allowing for the  mobilization of army reservists having  said that  we still haven't seen much detail this  was voted through  late last night in pretty chaotic  parliamentary  assembly it wasn't clear for a long time  if it would actually get the  numbered required number of votes it did  but whether this is just an option the  government is giving itself  for the case of further escalation with  russia or if we're going to see a direct  impact on everyday ukrainians lives we  still don't know and this is  what will come out of today really emily  in moscow  uh western nations are calling on russia  to release the ukrainian sailors and  ships that has seized is that likely to  happen  well terry it doesn't seem like there  will be a concession on that issue from  russia any time  soon especially because russia considers  that it captured  those vessels and those sailors  absolutely legally they  absolutely here in russia see that they  acted correctly and according to russian  and international law in fact the  russian side has very much been  categorizing what happened  as a ukrainian invasion um you have to  remember that russia considers the azov  sea to be  russian waters after the annexation of  crimea in 2014 which of course  ukraine does not recognize and uh russia  has also been categorizing ukraine as  the aggressor in this situation  yesterday we saw a statement  from the fsb the russian security  services saying that actually  um it was the ukrainian vessels that  pointed their guns  at the russian vessels first um and  threatened them um so very much uh  russia categorizing  ukraine as being in the wrong and  ukraine as being the aggressor here so i  don't think any concessions on the crew  and the vessels  that were captured anytime soon terry  so nick as emily points out russia  regards what happened there  as a ukrainian invasion explain to us  how the disputed waterway we're talking  about here is shared  and why it's so important to ukraine  well terry obviously before the russian  annexation of crimea in 2014  you had ukrainian controlled crimea on  the western side of the strait and then  the russian mainland on the eastern side  and they shared control  over this waterway to the sea of azov  which then also  obviously is shared by ukraine by  um ukrainian ports like mariupol and  then the russian side  since russia took over crimea basically  they have also  had de facto control over access to sea  of azov  something that ukraine and the west  obviously do not recognize but this is  crucial not just for crimea this is not  just something  about sovereignty in an abstract sense  this is also about  donbass about the other conflict that  ukraine has with russia  because tens of thousands of jobs depend  on the ports on the sea of azov  being able to get trade in and out if  it's this seed turns into a military  zone  with no real access from outside that  could really destabilize the situation  in the east of ukraine  where it had seemed in recent months  that things had become calmer and more  stable

